{% doc %}
  The lowest price currently on sale in a collection, in pence.

  Returned as a bare number so the caller decides how to show it. Zero means
  nothing in that collection can be bought right now, which is the caller's cue
  to fall back rather than print "from £0.00".

  This exists so a price written into a sentence ("Starter Boxes begin at...")
  comes from the products themselves. A figure typed into the theme editor is a
  second copy of a price, and a second copy of a price is one that eventually
  disagrees with the checkout.

  Out of stock products are skipped: a price nobody can pay is not a price the
  shop should be advertising.

  It walks the collection, so it is not free. Callers guard it behind a
  condition rather than running it on every page.

  @param {string} handle - the collection handle to look in
  @param {number} [max] - how many products to walk, default 50

  @example
  {% capture pence %}{% render 'collection-min-price', handle: 'starter-boxes' %}{% endcapture %}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign walk = max | default: 50
  assign source = collections[handle]
  assign lowest = 0

  for item in source.products limit: walk
    if item.available
      if lowest == 0 or item.price < lowest
        assign lowest = item.price
      endif
    endif
  endfor

  echo lowest
-%}
